I am trying to put some order in my code because I need to implement a huge application and I am creating functions from chunks of code to improve readibility and favour reuse.

I have an issue when trying to do so with renderTable.

My original call cannot be fully converted to a function, the renderTable must stay outside:
  renderResults <- function(objectives) {
#    renderTable({
      d<-data.frame(Result=objectives)
      d<-format(d,big.mark=",", scientific=FALSE)
      d[,1]<-paste('$',d[,1])
      d[1,]<-paste("<strong>",d[1,],"</strong>")
      rownames(d)[1]<-paste("<strong>",rownames(d)[1],"</strong>")
      d
#    }, align='lr',sanitize.text.function = function(x) x)
  }

and be called:
  output$results <-
  renderTable({
    renderResults(scalc()$Objectives)
  }, align='lr',sanitize.text.function = function(x) x)

what is not exactly what I wanted because I need to set the options each time.

I have also tried with observe aassuming that someone needs to check when scalc(), that is reactive, changes but they don't work either.

Is there any way to improve this? What is so special on renderXXX functions that this doesn't work? I guess is related to scoping but I don't get it.

I have managed to solve it including output$results generation inside scalc() but only works if I keep another output that I generate from scalc() using renderDataTable outside scalc(). If both outputs are generated inside then I get no output. There are only two outputs in the app. The second output generated with renderDataTable depends on another reactive variable: change() Both variables get triggered from the same button. I suspect the outputs are by default also reactive element that requires a trigger.

I know it's a bit confusing, I can try to prepare a minimum code that helps to reproduce the problem.

I am using R 3.1.1 on R studio 0.98, with shiny 0.10.0 and Windows 7

